Websites can protect against various vulnerabilities related to displaying images using several security measures. Below are some common techniques:

1. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: CSP is a HTTP header that helps prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) and data injection attacks by specifying which sources of content are allowed to be executed on the website.

```html
<!-- Example of setting Content Security Policy in an HTML meta tag -->
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' https://example.com;">
```

2. **Image URL Validation**: Ensure that image URLs are validated to prevent injection of malicious content or unauthorized access.

```php
// Example of validating image URLs in PHP
function validateImageUrl($url) {
    $allowedDomains = ['https://example.com', 'https://secureimages.org'];
    $parsedUrl = parse_url($url);

    if (in_array($parsedUrl['host'], $allowedDomains)) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

$imageUrl = "https://example.com/image.jpg";
if (validateImageUrl($imageUrl)) {
    echo '<img src="' . htmlspecialchars($imageUrl) . '" alt="Valid Image">';
}
```

3. **Output Encoding**: Always encode user-generated content to prevent XSS attacks when displaying it on the webpage.

```php
// Example of output encoding in PHP
$userInput = "<script>alert('XSS');</script>";
$safeOutput = htmlspecialchars($userInput, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');
echo $safeOutput;
```

4. **Using HTTPS**: Ensure that all images are served over HTTPS to prevent Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks and protect image data in transit.

```html
<!-- Example of using HTTPS for an image source -->
<img src="https://example.com/image.jpg" alt="Secure Image">
```

5. **Image Upload Restrictions**: If users can upload images, restrict file types to common formats like JPEG, PNG, GIF, etc., and scan uploaded files for malware.

```php
// Example of checking file type in PHP
if (isset($_FILES['image']) && $_FILES['image']['error'] == UPLOAD_ERR_OK) {
    $fileTmpPath = $_FILES['image']['tmp_name'];
    $fileName = basename($_FILES['image']['name']);
    $fileType = strtolower(pathinfo($fileName, PATHINFO_EXTENSION));

    $allowedTypes = ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'gif'];
    if (in_array($fileType, $allowedTypes)) {
        // Process the file upload
    } else {
        echo "Invalid file type.";
    }
}
```

6. **Rate Limiting**: Implement rate limiting to prevent denial of service attacks by limiting the number of image requests from a single IP address within a certain time frame.

```php
// Example of simple rate limiting in PHP
$limit = 10; // Number of requests allowed per minute
$expireTime = 60; // Time in seconds

if (isset($_SESSION['imageRequestCount'])) {
    $requestCount = $_SESSION['imageRequestCount'];
    if ($requestCount >= $limit) {
        echo "Too many requests. Try again later.";
        exit;
    } else {
        $_SESSION['imageRequestCount'] += 1;
    }
} else {
    $_SESSION['imageRequestCount'] = 1;
    $_SESSION['imageRequestTime'] = time();
}

// Reset the count after a minute
if (isset($_SESSION['imageRequestTime']) && (time() - $_SESSION['imageRequestTime']) > $expireTime) {
    unset($_SESSION['imageRequestCount']);
    unset($_SESSION['imageRequestTime']);
}
```

Implementing these security measures can significantly reduce the risk of vulnerabilities when displaying images on a website.

